{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 260,
    "dDurationMs": 4509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you look up at the night sky in any direction,\npast all the stars and more stars and galaxies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4769,
    "dDurationMs": 4411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and superclusters of galaxies… you will\nsee light that has been traveling for 13.7"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9180,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "billion years to reach earth. It's the oldest\nand most primeval light in the universe, a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13780,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "picture of our cosmos in its hot younger years,\nand it's called the \"Cosmic Background Radiation,\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19060,
    "dDurationMs": 2910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course you can't really see this light\nwith your naked eyes, because it's in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21970,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "microwave band of the electromagnetic spectrum.\nBut it is visible to radios and radio telescopes,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27200,
    "dDurationMs": 3850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even makes up a small portion of the 'salt\nand pepper' on an analog TV."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31050,
    "dDurationMs": 4059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Where does this luminescent background come\nfrom? Well, just after the big bang, the entire"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35109,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe was still so small it would have\nbeen very dense, scorchingly hot, and, because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39399,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it hadn't yet had time to get rough and uneven,\nit would also have been scrumptiously smooth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44059,
    "dDurationMs": 3701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a while, things would have been so sweltering\nthat electrons didn't settle down as parts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47760,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of atoms or molecules but instead roamed freely\nin a kind of red-hot cosmic soup. That soup"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52760,
    "dDurationMs": 4139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would have had lots of light bouncing around\nit, too, scattering off of electrons and protons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56899,
    "dDurationMs": 1971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like a hall of mirrors or the interior of\nthe sun..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58870,
    "dDurationMs": 3439,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, as the universe expanded, there was\nless and less energy to be had in any one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62309,
    "dDurationMs": 4132,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "place.  And when things had cooled to just\nbelow the temperature of the sun, pairs of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66441,
    "dDurationMs": 3559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electrons and protons no longer had the energy\nto resist each other and they fell into the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70000,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electromagnetic embrace we call the hydrogen\natom. These electrons were so enamored by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74310,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their new proton love interests that they\neffectively began to ignore all the light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77500,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bouncing around them. So, with fewer free\nelectrons for light to interact with, the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81110,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe suddenly became transparent, and\nall the pent-up light was sent forth in whatever"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85120,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "direction it had been headed after its last\nscattering, doomed to travel alone and unnoticed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89740,
    "dDurationMs": 3790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the cosmos. That is, until it bumps\ninto something solid…"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93530,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we finally see it here on earth, this\nlight has been stretched so much by the thirteen-billion-year"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97960,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansion of space, that, like a record slowing\ndown, its frequency and color have shifted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103770,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the original sunlight-white all the way\nto cool microwaves. Thus, it's often called"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108240,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the \"cosmic Microwave background radiation\",\nor CMB. And just as we can tell the temperature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113090,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a red or white-hot iron from its glow,\nthis light tells us the temperature of empty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space: currently around 2.725 degrees Kelvin,\nor minus 270 degrees Celsius."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122600,
    "dDurationMs": 5689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, the universe isn't exactly 2.725\nKelvin in every direction… if we look closely,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128289,
    "dDurationMs": 4090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are small and seemingly random but noticeable\nbumps all over the place, kind of like milk"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132379,
    "dDurationMs": 4481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's starting to curdle. Our best understanding\nis that these cosmic curds formed as quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136860,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluctuations in the otherwise creamy infant\nuniverse, and then began to coagulate as the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141130,
    "dDurationMs": 4109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe cooled and expanded.\nIt's hard to overstate just how small, or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145239,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "un-bumpy, these fluctuations of temperature\nand density were to begin with - the hot or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149409,
    "dDurationMs": 4121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cold spots were hotter or colder than their\nsurroundings by a factor of about one in a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153530,
    "dDurationMs": 4069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hundred thousand - that's like noticing that\na bacteria makes a beach ball bigger."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157599,
    "dDurationMs": 3881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But, while this clumping of the universe initially\nresulted in small variations like the ones"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161480,
    "dDurationMs": 5409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we see in the CMB, later on the chunky curds\nof primordial soup attracted each other gravitationally,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166889,
    "dDurationMs": 3661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and they ultimately coagulated and coalesced\nto form all of the massive structures in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170550,
    "dDurationMs": 4689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe that we see today, like planets,\nstars, galaxies, and superclusters of galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175239,
    "dDurationMs": 3441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So when we look up at the night sky past those\ngalaxies and see the ancient light of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178680,
    "dDurationMs": 4449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmic microwave background radiation, we're\nliterally seeing the starting point, the proverbial"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183129,
    "dDurationMs": 4681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cream, if you will, from which the starry\ncurds of the universe congealed. Or quite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187810,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simply, proof that the moon really is made\nof cheese."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193040,
    "dDurationMs": 4869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To give you a more complete experience of\nhow awesome the cosmic background radiation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197909,
    "dDurationMs": 3741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is, we've made an ADVENTURE! map showing it\nas if it were the out-of-this-world geography"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201650,
    "dDurationMs": 4259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of your favorite fantasy series - except it's\nreally the first picture taken of our universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205909,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a baby! We've included pictures of constellations\nand galaxies with the map, plus an overlay"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210309,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of what the sky looks like in infrared. And\nif you're feeling imperial, you can help name"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214049,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the oceans and mountains on our map. Head\nover to bigbangregistry.com to start exploring!"
    } ]
  } ]
}
